@meta
id: 22-13
title: Insert on every line at once
chapter: 22
type: lesson
skills: visual-block, block-insert
par: 27
minutes: 5
vscode: Alt+click ten times, type export, hope you did not miss one
open: constants.ts

@brief
This is the lesson the chapter is named for.

With a block selected, `I` opens insert mode at the block's left edge
on the *first* line only. You type, and nothing happens on the other
lines. Then you press `Esc`, and whatever you typed appears on every
line of the block, in the same column. It looks broken until the `Esc`.
It is not.

The constants in `constants.ts` are needed by the resolver now. Export
all ten of them: every `const` line gets `export ` in front of it. The
comment at the top and the blank line are not part of it.

This is what Alt+click multi-cursor does in VS Code, except that it
needs no mouse, it does not care whether the lines are on screen, and
it works the same on ten lines and on a thousand.

@keys
| `Ctrl-V` | start a block |
| `I` | insert at the left edge of the block, on every line |
| `Esc` | the moment the text is copied to the other lines |

@hints
Get to the first `const`. `Ctrl-V` and nine `j` — the block only needs
to be one column wide, because `I` only cares about the left edge.
--
`I`, type `export ` with its trailing space, and watch only the first
line change. Then `Esc`.
--
`/const` `Enter`, `Ctrl-V`, `9j`, `Iexport `, `Esc`.

@solution
    /const<Enter> Ctrl-V 9 j I export<Space> <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Twenty-three keystrokes, eight of them the word. `:'<,'>normal Iexport `
from chapter 15 does this too, and `:s/^/export /` does as well. Block
`I` is the one people actually use, because the block is visible
before you commit, and because the same block takes `A`, `c`, `r` and
`d` too — the next lessons.

The one-line-until-`Esc` behaviour catches everyone once. If you leave
insert mode with `Ctrl-C` instead of `Esc`, the text stays on
the first line only.

@start constants.ts
// Money is stored in pence. Windows are in days.

const REFUND_WINDOW_DAYS = 30;
const REFUND_MINIMUM_PENCE = 100;
const REFUND_MAXIMUM_PENCE = 500_000;
const ORDER_CUTOFF_HOUR = 14;
const ORDER_MAX_LINES = 50;
const PAYMENT_RETRY_LIMIT = 3;
const PAYMENT_RETRY_BACKOFF_MS = 10_000;
const PAYMENT_CAPTURE_TIMEOUT_MS = 30_000;
const EMAIL_RETRY_LIMIT = 5;
const EMAIL_FROM = 'orders@example.com';

@expected constants.ts
// Money is stored in pence. Windows are in days.

export const REFUND_WINDOW_DAYS = 30;
export const REFUND_MINIMUM_PENCE = 100;
export const REFUND_MAXIMUM_PENCE = 500_000;
export const ORDER_CUTOFF_HOUR = 14;
export const ORDER_MAX_LINES = 50;
export const PAYMENT_RETRY_LIMIT = 3;
export const PAYMENT_RETRY_BACKOFF_MS = 10_000;
export const PAYMENT_CAPTURE_TIMEOUT_MS = 30_000;
export const EMAIL_RETRY_LIMIT = 5;
export const EMAIL_FROM = 'orders@example.com';
